#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199277} :activity/id #uuid "4228fb68-d9aa-54a5-8d69-9202b473f5c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321 :topic/id #uuid "ebd1a7e8-239e-581a-aa6c-f96c639be509" :topic/math-academy-id 4135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198011 :question/id #uuid "3b27595a-5d6d-5ea3-92d2-6de7895c19fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-11153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050087}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389105}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "9c75dcfe-d31b-552f-8b7f-bb0e2c83fbe1" :step/math-academy-id 42408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198017 :question/id #uuid "b9d1a634-f1ef-5130-83ca-f3a555027699" :question/math-academy-id "e-12332"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389153}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "8bab0285-041c-5645-bfb0-d1af1dccc616" :step/math-academy-id 44184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199463 :tutorial/content "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nA sketch of $\\Delta$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ onto the $xy$-plane, we need to transform each side (bottom, top, left, and right) of $\\Delta$ separately: \n\n![](images/06/062a3d1f13e8566770118650c92231ebe17f5eba2b4ae8d8b41722e33a72baff.png)\n\nIt can be shown that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$ So, let's proceed to find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by mapping these four sides." :tutorial/id #uuid "89fbd7db-7e0a-5047-9be9-1a886c8970f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7615 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e182722-189e-524f-a581-82e71805ddc4" :step/math-academy-id 44217 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198019 :question/id #uuid "4eaf3262-b6e3-57bc-9662-42ab52626ee9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389091}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "c6da0258-e96c-5bbb-91d8-5c5aab571523" :step/math-academy-id 44222 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199464 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following transformation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v), \\qquad u > 0, \\quad 0\\leq v < 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $u = r$ and $v = \\theta,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(r,\\theta) \\to (r \\cos \\theta, \\, r \\sin \\theta), \\qquad r > 0, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that these are the usual formulas that we use to write the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta)$ of a point in the plane in Cartesian $(x,y)$ coordinates. \n\nThis gives us a fresh perspective of the meaning behind the usual $(r,\\theta)\\to(x,y)$ formulas:\n\n- Firstly, they describe a transformation that maps a region $\\Delta$ in the $(r,\\theta)$ plane (often a rectangle) to another region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Similarly, the inverse transformation (which always exists) maps a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane to a region $\\Delta$ in the $(r,\\theta)$ plane.\n\nThis idea becomes important later on. But, broadly speaking, many problems become easier after transforming them to another coordinate system where the domain under consideration is rectangular." :tutorial/id #uuid "49541498-bef0-5f43-a3fc-95ac9db70e7e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7618 :tutorial/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "c28b3ebd-437b-5803-87ec-1e04e0f5c191" :step/math-academy-id 44223 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198026 :question/id #uuid "f4539253-b127-5d96-a331-3b357dc08a9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389112}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "616ce947-0b5d-5274-955e-7ae934283b51" :step/math-academy-id 44308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199475 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8be5595b-5342-5f9e-9e69-9591c32d7780" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7670 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment"} :step/id #uuid "2349a1aa-a4dc-5683-a96c-be82f8b7ab2e" :step/math-academy-id 44555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199476 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "74b0f131-a207-5518-ade5-32ceacbf2263" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7671 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment"} :step/id #uuid "21530539-0f1c-512e-92ce-ac7f86f8be41" :step/math-academy-id 44556 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199267}}] :activity/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198481}]}
